WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.350
- I have to say this your
wife, the last time she was on,

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.970
we were talking about music
and she's so into music.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.470
So I didn't get to ask her.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.970
Do you, did you ever get
into music or musicals

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.450
or do any kind of theater like that?

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.150
- No, I could not sing my
way out of a paper bag.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.350
I did though at Carnegie

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:21.000
get cast in the, it was a,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.610
I think a director's master's project.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.527
Do you remember the musical Godspell?

00:00:25.527 --> 00:00:26.360
- Yeah.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.193
- Right.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:31.500
And Godspell was created
there and I was in it.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.880
I got cast in it and it was just horrible.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.180
And I mean, it really,
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.000
it didn't come together.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.760
It didn't come together
until like the last night,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.730
but halfway through the rehearsal process,

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.930
I got Bell's palsy.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:47.763
- What?
- You know what

00:00:47.763 --> 00:00:48.596
Bell's palsy is?

00:00:48.596 --> 00:00:49.530
It's like, you know,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.600
you take a pencil and you draw
down the center of your face

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.930
and one side literally is paralyzed.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.640
So my reaction to
getting Bell's palsy was,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.235
oh, thank God I'm out of this turkey.

00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:05.260
(Kelly and audience laugh)
- Oh my God,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.590
I love the silver lining.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.110
Oh my God, did that freak you out?

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.504
That I don't, what?

00:01:10.504 --> 00:01:12.753
Doesn't that kind of
come and go sometimes?

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.020
- Yeah.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:15.853
Yeah,

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.693
I was lucky.

00:01:17.693 --> 00:01:20.290
I was lucky, but it
was, it was so humbling.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.940
You had to tape the side that drooped

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.530
into a smile at night and sleep, you know,

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.743
with this Scotch Tape doing this, or you,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.440
I was a smoker back then, so you'd smoke.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.090
And one eye would blink normally,

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.890
and the other one would be like ahh.

00:01:38.751 --> 00:01:41.280
(Kelly and audience laugh)

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.070
- Oh my gosh, that's horrible.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.720
I really wanna see your one
man show about your life.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:51.060
So, Holly, you won an
Oscar for The Piano, but,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.250
and you play the piano beautifully,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.137
but you don't like to play
in front of people, right?

00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:55.543
- No, no, no, no.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:00.430
I grew up playing the piano
and I totally loved playing.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.190
It was a real passion.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.920
But I can never play in front of people,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.950
it made me too nervous.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.950
So when we, when we
were shooting the movie,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.870
The Piano, I just had the
piano on the set all the time.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.978
It was part of my contract that
the piano was always there.

00:02:18.978 --> 00:02:22.220
So that any time that when the crew

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.110
was setting up for the next shot,

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.630
I would play all of the time.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.270
I would play in front of them,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.730
It was like background noise for them.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.620
They didn't pay any attention to me.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.900
And that kind of freed me

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.030
to be able to play in front of them,

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.400
because they were gonna
be my only audience

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.280
when we were actually rolling,
and I was doing the songs.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.860
So I got used to them,
they got used to me.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.480
Sometimes they would actually
request different songs

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:51.550
while they were setting up,

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:52.383
or somebody would say,

00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:53.880
"Oh wow, that sounded really good."

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.238
And that gave me the confidence

00:02:56.238 --> 00:03:01.238
to be able to perform in
front of that small audience,

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.580
which was that incredible crew.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.413
- Yeah, I was gonna say,

00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:07.853
it's usually more intimidating
in a small setting like that,

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:09.750
to mess up or anything.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.450
Do you think it helped because you're,

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.059
you're in character,
so you don't feel like

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:14.892
it's you?
- Did you always

00:03:14.892 --> 00:03:15.933
like to sing in front of people?

00:03:15.933 --> 00:03:19.240
Were you always able to just like,

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.149
was that something

00:03:20.149 --> 00:03:22.480
you always felt free about?
- Here's the thing,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.600
I've never been nervous on
stage singing, but I did,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.230
and my musical director is
probably laughing over there.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.390
He's taught me a couple
of my songs on piano

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.340
that I was gonna do on tour at
different points or whatever.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.607
And I will sit in my house and
I will play and I will sing,

00:03:34.607 --> 00:03:36.470
and it will be fine, it'll be beautiful.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.830
Like, well, not great.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.310
I just mean it will be good.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.710
And then once we get to
rehearsal, I just freak out,

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.650
I'd be like, "Nope, I'm just gonna sing."

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.800
I don't even know why I did all that work,

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:47.710
I'm never gonna do it.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:49.169
Like, I just, I don't know why,

00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:51.580
I've never been nervous singing,

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.960
so I don't know how to deal
with the nerves of doing,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.710
'cause I've also played guitar on tour,

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:00.200
but I'm pretty sure they just
like, you know, turn me down,

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.610
like had the other band up,
layer the other members up.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.443
But I just,

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:06.870
I'm very focused,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.430
like hyper-focused, and
I'm such a storyteller.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.850
Like, I love a story, I
love lyrics, I love singing

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.040
that I don't like to be distracted

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.400
in front of people when I'm performing.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.030
But the difference is I'm playing me,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.640
like, it's me in real life.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.220
You're playing like in The Pianos,

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.080
in The Piano, you're playing someone else.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.190
So, is that kind of easier

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.400
because it's a little bit of like,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.360
you know, removal of yourself?

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:29.320
- Yes.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.020
I think that that definitely
makes it somewhat easier.

